  ...continue for full length

Rules for the grid:

  • No same retention TYPE used in 3 consecutive blocks.
  • The first 5 blocks (first 2.5 minutes) must each be a DIFFERENT type.
  • Blocks 1, 2, and 3 must ALL be UNANSWERED QUESTION or REVEALED INFO —
    this is the critical drop-off window.
  • Every 6 blocks (3 minutes), insert a FULL EMOTIONAL RESET:
    a moment of human cost, sacrifice, or quiet before the next escalation.
  • The climax window (final 20% of script) must alternate:
    PROCEDURAL MOMENTUM → REVEALED INFO → EMOTIONAL → PROCEDURAL → PAYOFF.

================================================================================
THE 7 DROP-OFF DANGER ZONES — AVOID ALL OF THEM
================================================================================

These are the moments viewers leave. Engineer around each one:

DANGER ZONE 1 — THE FIRST 15 SECONDS
  Problem: Opening is too slow, generic, or biographical.
  Fix: Line 1 = date + place + impossible situation. No exceptions.
  Test: If line 1 could open a Wikipedia article, rewrite it.

DANGER ZONE 2 — THE 90-SECOND BIOGRAPHY SWAMP
  Problem: Too much backstory before the main action resumes.
  Fix: Backstory maximum = 4 consecutive sentences before returning
       to forward momentum. Use "लेकिन उसी दौरान..." to escape.
  Test: Count backstory sentences. If >4 in a row → interrupt with
        a present-tense tension reminder.

DANGER ZONE 3 — THE FLAT MIDDLE (minutes 4–8)
  Problem: Plot is established, viewer confidence drops.
  Fix: Minutes 4–8 must contain the story's SECOND BIGGEST surprise
       — not the climax, but a revelation that reframes everything.
  Test: Identify what the viewer thinks they know at minute 4.
        Shatter one assumption by minute 6.

DANGER ZONE 4 — THE REPETITIVE TRANSITION LOOP
  Problem: Same phrase used to signal tension 3+ times.
  Fix: Transition phrase bank (see below). No phrase used >2 times.
  Test: Search script for "लेकिन कहानी यहीं खत्म नहीं होती" —
        if it appears more than twice, replace the extras.

DANGER ZONE 5 — THE STRATEGY LECTURE
  Problem: Planning scenes become abstract talking-head narration.
  Fix: Every strategy must be shown through a physical object or action:
       map, satellite image, file, countdown clock, movement route.
       Max 3 consecutive sentences of pure strategy before a visual anchor.
  Test: Find any block of strategy narration. Does an animator know
        exactly what to draw? If not, add the visual anchor.

DANGER ZONE 6 — THE VILLAIN SHRINK
  Problem: The enemy/obstacle is defeated too easily, making victory feel cheap.
  Fix: The enemy must do something SMART or DANGEROUS in the second half.
       They must appear to be winning at least once after the midpoint.
  Test: What is the enemy's best moment? If they have no best moment → add one.

DANGER ZONE 7 — THE COLD LANDING
  Problem: The ending arrives without emotional preparation.
  Fix: The final 10% of the script must do 4 things in order:
       (a) Payoff the cold open's unanswered question
       (b) Name the human cost (even briefly)
       (c) State the "naya Bharat" meaning
       (d) Leave one open question for the comments
  Test: Does the last paragraph make the viewer feel something AND think
        something? Both must be true.

